Great.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.017
So in order to set some common
ground, I know some of you

00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:54.475
are probably security
professionals

00:00:54.475 --> 00:00:56.330
and this might seem a
little bit repetitive.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:58.850
But I want to make sure that
everyone is on the same page.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.700
So I want to start by
talking and answering

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.420
a very simple question.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.870
What is a secret?

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.450
So for the purpose of
this talk, a secret

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.290
is a credential, a
configuration, an API key,

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.330
or some other piece
of information

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.790
that is required for an
application either at build

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.488
time or at runtime.

00:01:17.488 --> 00:01:19.780
So some examples here might
be like a database username

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.660
and password, a Twitter
API key, any type

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.120
of secret or
sensitive information

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.940
that the application
needs to run.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:32.760
And that begs the next question,
which is why do we care.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.460
Why should we just
not use social media

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.107
as our secrets management tool?

00:01:36.107 --> 00:01:38.690
Why don't you store your secrets
in publicly available tweets?

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.050
Well, it turns out that secrets
are an attractive target

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.090
for attackers.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.090
Secrets often protect
sensitive information,

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.055
like personally
identifiable information,

00:01:49.055 --> 00:01:50.930
credit card numbers,
Social Security numbers,

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.160
passport numbers, or they
protect access to business data

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.657
business intelligence data
like analytics and reports

00:01:57.657 --> 00:01:59.990
that could damage your business
or give your competitors

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:02.410
an advantage.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.200
But then you couple that
with how easily credentials

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:05.920
are leaked.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.740
If we look at any of the
recent security incidents,

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:14.260
we've seen access keys leaked
in a public repository because

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.570
of a small mistake
from a developer.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.900
We've seen improperly
secured access

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.590
to a dashboard or an interface
allowing privilege escalation.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:27.760
It's very easy for us to
leak these credentials

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.380
and expose secrets
unintentionally.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.570
And then third, most
secrets actually

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:36.340
have more permission
than they need,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.280
particularly when we're
talking about IAM credentials

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.200
or identity and access
management credentials.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.260
And that's because when
you're a large startup,

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.965
it's just much easier to
give everyone root access,

00:02:46.965 --> 00:02:48.340
because I don't
know what they're

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.780
going to need in a week or two.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:49.990
Right.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:51.010
I don't want to have
to go through that.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.420
I need to move quickly.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.400
And then oftentimes
security is an afterthought.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.370
So when you combine
these three things,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.520
you have something that accesses
customer data or business

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.930
data that's easily leaked and
has overly broad permissions.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:09.080
It's an attacker's
like best playground.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.480
So this is why we
need to invest effort

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.130
in protecting our secrets.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:18.270
And there are four key ways in
which we protect secrets today.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.580
The first is auditing.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.900
We verify all of
the use and access

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:28.460
of individual secrets in some
type of centralized system.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.360
This allows us to apply anomaly
detection or machine learning

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.670
to find out whenever a secret is
being accessed out of the norm.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.860
Second, we use encryption.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.590
All of our data should
be encrypted in transit

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.610
with TLS and at rest with
the most secure algorithm

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.570
for your use case
and your industry.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:53.320
Third, we should be rotating
both secrets and encryption

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:55.120
keys regularly.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.090
In addition to a regular
schedule of rotation,

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.520
we need a break glass procedure.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.010
What happens if right
now we leak a credential?

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.200
How quickly can we
rotate that credential

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.820
across our infrastructure?

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.740
Right now we leak an API key.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.990
How quickly can we revoke
and replace that API key?

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:14.170
So we need both
planned rotation,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.019
which decreases our
surface area for an attack,

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:21.950
and we need immediate rotation
in the event of an emergency.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.720
And fourth, isolation is key.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.770
We want to separate where
secrets are stored from where

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.510
secrets are accessed.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.990
That way an attacker has to
compromise multiple systems

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:36.050
and escalate privilege multiple
times in order to gain access

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:39.100
to that plaintext information.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.720
For the purposes
of this talk, we're

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.780
going to be focusing on that
second component, specifically

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.200
encryption.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.640
Now that doesn't mean that the
other three are less important,

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.490
but I only have 40
minutes so we're

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:55.210
going to focus on encryption.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.630
There are multiple
layers of encryption.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.790
You might have things like
encrypted file systems

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:03.945
like FileVault, self-encrypting
hard drives, et cetera.

00:05:03.945 --> 00:05:05.820
But you might also have,
say, a machine level

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.680
encryption, or service
level encryption,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.900
or app layer encryption.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.810
And we recommend that you
always use at least two layers

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.750
of encryption for your data.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.300
Now on Google Cloud,
all of your data

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.990
is encrypted at rest by default.
No configuration necessary.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:24.530
But you may want to apply
additional layers of encryption

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.630
to your data.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.450
Specifically, you may want
to leverage application layer

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.080
encryption.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.940
Application layer
encryption is applied

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.560
at one of the earliest possible
stages of the data's lifecycle.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.170
Secrets are encrypted before
they leave your application

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.560
or service with keys
or encryption tokens

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.360
that only your
application knows.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.720
So in this way only
your application

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.480
or only the critical
components of your system

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.540
see the plaintext value.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.277
This enables very granular
protection of data.

00:05:57.277 --> 00:05:58.860
You can use a different
encryption key

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.840
for each piece of customer data.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.880
That way if an attacker
is able to brute force

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.280
that encryption key
or the decryption key,

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.810
that same key will not work for
another piece of customer data.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.880
And third, this
protects the data

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.290
as it moves
throughout the system.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.430
So if I have a hard drive and
that hard drive is encrypted,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.397
if I mount that hard drive
and decrypt it somewhere,

00:06:20.397 --> 00:06:22.230
if an attacker has
access to that hard drive

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.780
they have access to all
of the plaintext data now.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.090
But by leveraging
application layer encryption,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.090
as I move my secret data
throughout the system,

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.940
it remains encrypted regardless
of the underlying storage

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.940
mechanism, whether that's
memory, a hard disk, or a thumb

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.310
drive.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.250
In the context of Kubernetes,
application layer encryption

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.680
means that the
Kubernetes API server

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.590
is responsible for performing
the crypto operations.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.360
So in addition to
your application,

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.090
your pod, your service,
the Kubernetes API server

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.960
also has privilege to see
those plaintext secrets.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.130
And we advocate for
defense in depth.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.960
Again, use multiple
layers of encryption.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.290
Use application layer encryption
combined with operating system

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.900
layer encryption, things like
secure boot and hard drive

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.110
encryption, et cetera.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:12.460
So now let's talk
about Kubernetes.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:15.190
The Kubernetes
default installation

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.540
is not encrypted by
default. So today,

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:21.300
if you were to download
the open source

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.650
distribution of Kubernetes and
start up a Kubernetes cluster,

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.230
regardless of cloud provider,
regardless of infrastructure,

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:31.890
the underlying secrets
are not encrypted.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.620
That means that anyone
who has access to ETCD

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.880
or a backup of ETCD has access
to all of your plaintext--

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.635
all of your secrets
in plaintext.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.170
Let me show you what
that looks like.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:51.020
Here I have a credit
card or a piece of data.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.950
I'm using this image to
show that this is a secret.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.260
As a user or as
an application, I

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.350
give that piece of secret
data to the Kubernetes API

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.290
server when I say
kubectl- create secret

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.000
or the associated API call.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.130
The Kubernetes API server
then base64 encodes that data

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:14.050
and writes it to ETCD.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:14.920
I saw someone smile.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.210
They got my base64 joke.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:22.060
So the app submits the
secret to Kubernetes.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:26.320
The API server pushes that
into ETCD into plaintext.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.590
That means that if an
attacker has access

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.640
to the master node,
an online ETCD

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.090
server, or a backup
of the ETCD database,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.900
all of your Kubernetes secrets
are available in plaintext.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.510
Again, this is a default
Kubernetes installation,

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.760
regardless of cloud,
regardless of infrastructure,

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.960
the open source
distro out of the box.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.530
This is called encraption.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.890
Encraption is what happens
when you confuse encryption

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:54.473
with encoding.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:00.370
And my goal here is not
to make fun of Kubernetes.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:00.870
Right.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.440
Kubernetes is an
amazing product.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.930
And they've made great
strides to improve

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.400
the overall security.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.980
So while this is
the default setup,

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.768
let's talk about just how
easy it is to extract secrets

00:09:11.768 --> 00:09:13.810
and then I'll talk about
some of the improvements

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:16.090
that Kubernetes has
made to make it easier

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.200
to secure your secrets.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.150
So if we could
jump over to the--

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:20.560
wow, you're ahead of me.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.060
Perfect.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:27.130
Now I pull out my cheat
sheet because I'm only human.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:27.630
All right.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.047
So what I have here is I'm
using a minikube, which is just

00:09:30.047 --> 00:09:31.650
a local Kubernetes cluster.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.790
And I have the base default
installation of the latest

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:36.870
version of Kubernetes.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.370
And what I'm going
to do here is I'm

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:44.910
going to go into
this folder and then

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:46.660
I'm going to show you
the command that I'm

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:47.702
going to be running here.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.750
So I'm going to create a secret.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:57.090
I'm going to use this default
cluster that I've created.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:58.770
And then I'm going
to create a secret

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.320
where I have a key value pair.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.790
The user name is
sethvargo. and the password

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.760
is very secure
secret in leet speak.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.875
So let me go ahead
and run this now.

00:10:07.875 --> 00:10:12.590
So run bin
create-secret-default. And now

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:14.010
I've created that secret.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.220
So as I was showing before, this
is a default Kubernetes setup,

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.587
which means it's stored
in ETCD in plaintext.

00:10:19.587 --> 00:10:21.420
So let me show you what
that looks like if I

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:25.350
access the default secret now.

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:28.530
You'll notice that I get back
some additional metadata here.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:29.070
Right.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.410
I get back almost like
a binary representation.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.970
There's some additional
metadata in here.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.510
But most importantly
right there,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.990
I've highlighted on the screen,
that's the plaintext password.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:41.580
That's the plaintext username.

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.260
All of that information
that I supplied

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.010
is in plaintext in ETCD.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:47.730
Very easy for an attacker
to extract that information

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.737
and escalate privilege
in my system.

00:10:49.737 --> 00:10:51.820
So let's go ahead and jump
back to the slides now.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:56.210
Perfect.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.030
Now before we talk about
some of the improvements

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.910
that Kubernetes has made,
let's talk about this concept

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:04.460
called envelope encryption,
because it's very important,

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:08.480
because it's the underlying
principle for how Kubernetes

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.690
secrets encryption works.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:15.262
So envelope encryption is this
idea of keys all the way down

00:11:15.262 --> 00:11:16.970
or turtles all the
way down, if you will.

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:18.345
There are three
pieces that we're

00:11:18.345 --> 00:11:19.700
going to talk about here today.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.670
Again, we have our data, which
was represented by this credit

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.260
card icon.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.110
We have a data
encryption key or a deck

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.150
represented by a red key.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.880
And we have a key
encryption key or a kek

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:35.130
represented by a green key.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:39.480
Now, when we want
to encrypt data,

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:44.370
we take the data encryption
key, the red key,

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.700
and we encrypt the data.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:49.410
So we generate a key and we
encrypt the data with that key.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.500
And that produces
encrypted data that

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:57.040
is encrypted with our red
key, our data encryption key.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:59.440
Then we take our
key encryption key

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:03.360
and we encrypt our
data encryption key.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:04.830
So you'll notice
here on the slide

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.710
I have the credit card, which
is encrypted with the red key.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.730
And then I have the
data encryption key,

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:12.730
which is encrypted with the
green key encryption key.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:14.910
We then take both of
those pieces of data

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.700
and we put them together,
implementation details aside,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.180
and we store them side by side
in some type of storage back

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:23.380
end, file system, database,
whatever it might be.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.640
And the idea here is
that each piece of data

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.940
has its own encryption key.

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:30.240
We generate a new red
key, a new data encryption

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.260
key for each piece of data, and
that that data is maintained

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.870
by a-- is encrypted by a
key encryption key, which

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.710
has its own rotation schedule.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:42.950
When we want to
reverse the process,

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:46.070
the encrypted blob is
separated into its two parts,

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.040
again implementation details.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.130
The key encryption key decrypts
the data encryption key bits.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:57.403
That then gives us the
plaintext data encryption key.

00:12:57.403 --> 00:12:59.320
We then take the plaintext
data encryption key

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.780
and decrypt the remaining
bits to give us back

00:13:01.780 --> 00:13:05.220
the original plaintext data.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.230
This is a very
rudimentary and very quick

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:10.620
overview of how envelope
encryption works.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:11.600
The key here is--

00:13:14.282 --> 00:13:18.210
if y'all are not up
for jokes, it's fine.

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.030
The key here is that
each new piece of data

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:25.260
is encrypted with a new
unique randomly generated key

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.540
and then we have a
regular rotation schedule

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.710
for our key encryption keys.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:32.320
So maybe that's monthly,
or weekly, or quarterly

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:37.730
depending on your threat
model and your requirements.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:41.030
So there are some best practices
for envelope encryption.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:44.160
We want to generate unique
data encryption keys.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.620
So each piece of
data is encrypted

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:49.020
with its own high
entropy unique key.

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:51.890
This allows us to do really
cool things like crypto-shred.

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:53.960
So if we revoke our
key encryption key,

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.780
that top level key, all of
the data encryption keys

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.240
are now irrecoverable
by traditional means

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.990
because they were
encrypted with that key.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.950
Additionally, we can rotate
our key encryption keys on time

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:08.410
based intervals or in the
event of an emergency while we

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:12.060
rotate the data encryption keys
each time we modify the data.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.290
So this provides a
very scalable way

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.030
to do cryptographic operations.

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:22.180
So now, let's go
back to Kubernetes.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:25.330
So Kubernetes 1.7
made its first attempt

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:28.570
at solving this problem by
introducing a new configuration

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:33.120
option for encrypting secrets
at rest before they enter ETCD.

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:34.820
And that looks like this.

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:37.420
So this is what we call an
encryption configuration.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.730
And you'll notice inside
this encryption configuration

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:42.100
I'm specifying an
encryption provider.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:45.160
In this example, it's
an AES-CBC provider.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:49.940
And I'm giving it the basics for
encoded encryption key I take

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:51.590
this encryption configuration.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:52.940
It's a YAML file.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:56.750
I load it onto my master node
or my Kubernetes API server.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:59.870
I then start or restart
my Kubernetes API server

00:14:59.870 --> 00:15:03.200
by passing in this encryption
provider configuration option.

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.640
I give it the path
to that YAML file.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.000
And now we're good to go.

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:11.470
So let me show you what this
looks like in this new model.

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.400
So going back to our
original diagram,

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.800
I have my credit
card, my secret data.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.020
I give that to the
Kubernetes API server.

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:24.560
Again, the Kubernetes API server
does see the plaintext here.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.240
But the Kubernetes
API server then

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.840
delegates to that encryption
provider configuration.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:34.070
The encryption provider
configuration encrypts the data

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.900
before writing it to ETCD.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:40.830
So we have random
bytes of data in ETCD.

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.260
So this means if an
attacker had access

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.530
to an online ETCD database or
a backup of the ETCD database,

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:51.600
they would not be able
to recover the plaintext

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.233
by traditional means.

00:15:55.233 --> 00:15:56.650
But what if an
attacker had access

00:15:56.650 --> 00:16:01.330
to the master VM, a full disk
backup of the master node

00:16:01.330 --> 00:16:03.640
or the Kubernetes API server?

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:07.330
Well, if an attacker
has access to that,

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:11.050
that encryption configuration
is on that same node in a file.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.030
It's in that YAML file
that I showed earlier.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:17.020
So if an attacker is able
to gain access to the VM,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.532
they have access to the
keys that encrypted the data

00:16:19.532 --> 00:16:21.865
and you haven't actually
improved your security posture.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.350
So from a threat
modeling perspective,

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:27.970
this actually wasn't any better.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:30.010
But it was a step in
the right direction,

00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:32.060
because now the community
started thinking,

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.390
hey, we should actually
be encrypting our secrets

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:37.280
and this is the way
that we should do it.

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:39.560
So there are a number of
drawbacks with this approach.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:42.500
The first, you have to
generate your own keys.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.440
You need your own
high entropy source.

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:46.360
You need to understand
key algorithms.

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:48.207
You need to understand
what is it like.

00:16:48.207 --> 00:16:49.290
What are you going to use?

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:51.498
Are you going to use CBC or
are you going to use GCM?

00:16:51.498 --> 00:16:53.020
What type of key do you want?

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:56.053
After you create those keys,
you then need to manage them.

00:16:56.053 --> 00:16:57.970
You need to set up your
own rotation schedule.

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:00.270
You need to set up your
own revocation schedule.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.020
You need to be responsible
for making sure

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:03.430
that they're always online.

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:05.200
And each time you
update those keys,

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.480
you have to restart your
Kubernetes API server,

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.450
potentially causing downtime.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:12.800
As I said, you have to
rotate them on your own.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:14.700
You have to update that config.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.992
And if you're trying to get
rid of old encryption keys,

00:17:16.992 --> 00:17:20.480
you actually have to
re-encrypt all of your secrets.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:21.980
Another drawback
is that many of you

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:25.880
might be in organizations
or regulatory requirements

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:29.430
where you're relying on
HSMs to achieve PCI or HIPAA

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:30.650
compliance.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.740
And there's no way with the
Kubernetes 1.7 encryption

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:36.750
provider configuration to
leverage a hardware security

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:37.250
module.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:39.740
You can't leverage an HSM.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.510
So that was a non-starter
for some of those customers

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:45.590
who were depending on that.

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:47.620
And most importantly,
as I already said,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.140
the biggest drawback is
that those encryption keys

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:52.427
are stored in plaintext
in the file system.

00:17:52.427 --> 00:17:54.010
So from a threat
modeling perspective,

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:56.650
we haven't actually increased
our security posturing

00:17:56.650 --> 00:17:57.880
by very much.

00:17:57.880 --> 00:17:59.820
The keys are still
available in plaintext.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:01.900
And because the attacker
could just get the keys

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.180
and decrypt the data,
it's effectively

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:09.710
an [? O ?] of N operation to get
the plaintext data out of ETCD.

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:14.360
So Kubernetes 1.10 provides a
much more secure solution using

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:17.800
a new kind of secrets
provider, KMS.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.820
In this model,
Kubernetes delegates

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.280
encryption to a third
party campus provider

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:24.830
like Google Cloud
or HashiCorp Vault.

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:28.000
So let me show you
what that looks like.

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:31.200
Again, it's the same
encryption configuration.

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.565
But instead of specifying
the keys directly

00:18:33.565 --> 00:18:35.190
in this encryption
configuration, which

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:39.990
lives on disk, we instead
specify the plugin and the Unix

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:41.385
socket where that
plugin resides.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:47.800
We load that [? out ?] the
same way that we did before,

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:52.750
but now there's a third
party system in our flow.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:54.220
We have our secret data.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:56.680
Our secret data goes
to the API server.

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.590
The API server hits the
encryption configuration.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:03.430
The encryption configuration
then makes a unix socket call

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:08.200
to, in the case of Google Cloud
KMS, the Google Cloud APIs

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:11.590
to encrypt or decrypt this data.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:14.470
Google Cloud KMS then
returns encrypted cipher text

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:18.350
which is then stored in ETCD.

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:22.850
So even if an attacker has
access to the master nodes,

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:24.590
those encryption
keys are maintained

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:26.450
by an external system.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:28.670
An attacker would have to
compromise both the master

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:32.780
nodes, ETCD, and the
third party KMS system

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.180
in order to be able to
decrypt those values offline.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:40.060
But that might
sound complicated.

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:40.970
Right.

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:43.430
Like that's a lot of things.

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:45.960
And that interaction with Unix
sockets and file permissions,

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:50.010
that sounds difficult. But
the community has already

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:52.170
built a few of these for us.

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:56.632
So there is an open source
Google Cloud KMS plugin

00:19:56.632 --> 00:19:57.840
that Alex actually worked on.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:00.150
He's going to talk a little
bit about it in a second.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:01.920
There's one for Azure.

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.410
There's one for AWS.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:05.100
And there's one for
HashiCorp Vault.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.770
So the communities and the
various special interest groups

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.130
are working hard to make sure
that your favorite secrets

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:11.880
management solutions
and your favorite KMS

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:15.310
providers are supported
as external KMS plugins

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:16.444
for Kubernetes.

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:23.490
Now if you are on Google
Cloud specifically,

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:24.960
we actually just
launched a feature

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:27.930
into beta that does
all of this for you.

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:30.510
It is so easy that
I actually thought

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.163
the PM was lying to me
when she showed it to me.

00:20:33.163 --> 00:20:35.080
It was like, it just
can't be this easy, Maya.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.600
There's no way.

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:38.250
But it actually is just as easy.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:40.080
So this is a gcloud command.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.540
There's an associated API call.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.220
You give it three
pieces of information

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.780
when you create your cluster.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:49.140
You give it the KMS key
location, the KMS key ring,

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:51.070
and the KMS key.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:54.370
You're telling GKE, the
Google Kubernetes engine,

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.325
please encrypt my secrets
with this Cloud KMS key.

00:20:57.325 --> 00:20:59.200
And all of that setup
and all of those things

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:04.597
I showed you before, that's
all done for you automatically.

00:21:04.597 --> 00:21:06.180
But this begs an
interesting question,

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.970
which is, how does
Kubernetes get permission

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:11.580
to talk to the KMS provider
in the first place?

00:21:11.580 --> 00:21:14.550
There has to be some
exchange authentication.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:17.340
And this is where cloud
providers have an advantage,

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:19.440
because cloud providers
offer identity and access

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:22.750
management as a first class
part of their product portfolio.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:26.010
So IAM solves that first
secret problem or the last mile

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:26.970
problem.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:28.830
We delegate privilege
and access management

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:31.402
to the cloud provider's IAM.

00:21:31.402 --> 00:21:33.360
And then we have a nice
separation of concerns.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:36.870
The ETCD nodes don't need IAM
permission to talk to KMS.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:39.240
So even if someone
has access to the VM

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:40.980
on which ETCD is
running, they don't

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:43.020
have permission to
talk to Cloud KMS,

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:44.670
because ETCD doesn't need that.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.500
Only the Kubernetes
API server does.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.080
So with that, I'd like
to invite Alex back

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:53.270
on stage to talk a little
bit about how this works

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.760
under the hood on
GKE and the way

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.690
that we do application
layer encryption.

00:21:57.690 --> 00:21:59.060
So please give it up for Alex.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.880
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:07.010
ALEX TCHERNIAKHOVSKI:
Thank you, Seth.

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:10.177
So Seth is very
convincing, so I don't even

00:22:10.177 --> 00:22:11.510
think I need to be here anymore.

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:13.460
But I guess I need
to prove something

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:15.830
that it actually works.

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:18.680
So I'm hoping that by this
point in the presentation,

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:21.620
you are reasonably convinced
that storing secrets

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:25.370
in plaintext in ETCD, even
though the underlying disk is

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:27.320
encrypted, may not
be the best security

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:29.750
posture for your organization.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:31.760
And you're wondering,
how do I enable

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:33.890
this additional
layer of encryption

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:35.690
or additional
layer of protection

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:41.530
when I'm running Kubernetes
in Google Kubernetes Engine?

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:44.600
So I will show
this in this demo.

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:46.560
It's a two step process.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.460
The first step is we need to
create cryptographic keys.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.660
We need to create cryptographic
keys in Cloud KMS.

00:22:55.310 --> 00:22:58.880
You will find cryptographic
keys in the security subsection

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:01.400
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:02.960
When you land there,
you will notice

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:06.050
there is a concept
of key rings that

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:08.800
is introduced by Cloud KMS.

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:13.040
Key rings are analogous to
directories in the file system.

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:17.550
You can group together logically
related keys and, for example,

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:20.990
apply IAM policy at the
key ring level as opposed

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:24.560
to managing individual keys.

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:28.970
What you will also notice is
that key ring's allocation

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:29.630
specific.

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.692
They attach to a
specific location.

00:23:33.692 --> 00:23:35.400
And it is important
point for us to know,

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:39.220
because our Kubernetes
cluster will need

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:41.560
to communicate with Cloud KMS.

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:44.050
And basically, whenever--
and we want that connection

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:45.620
to be as quick as possible.

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:48.580
So you want to [? co-locate ?]
your Kubernetes clusters

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:53.030
and your cloud KMS keys
in the same region.

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:54.240
OK.

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:57.990
So let me create a key.

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.810
Let's try to find
a meaningful name.

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:13.942
And you know what, the
defaults are just fine for us.

00:24:13.942 --> 00:24:15.400
Before I hit Create,
the only thing

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:18.130
I will point out to make
the previous discussion

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:20.440
about key rotation a
little bit more concrete

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:23.560
is this parameter
here, rotation period,

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.520
which is by default 90 days.

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:29.050
But you can see that you can
set it to whatever makes sense

00:24:29.050 --> 00:24:31.030
to your organization.

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:31.530
OK.

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:35.620
So let me go ahead
and click Create.

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:38.830
All right.

00:24:38.830 --> 00:24:41.500
So that takes care of
the prerequisite work.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:45.660
And now I'm ready to switch
to Kubernetes engine.

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:47.185
And I will create my cluster.

00:24:53.502 --> 00:24:55.210
In order to enable
this [? officially, ?]

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:58.210
we need to expand availability,
networking, security,

00:24:58.210 --> 00:24:59.800
and additional features section.

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:06.800
And I need to scroll down
to security subsection

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:12.250
and check Enable Application
Layer Encryption.

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.940
So just a quick
note on terminology.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:18.480
In this talk, we use the
term ETCD level encryption

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:19.160
quite a bit.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:20.880
And I think it's a
very pragmatic way

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.570
of thinking about it.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:25.410
But the reality is that
ETCD is an implementation

00:25:25.410 --> 00:25:27.060
detail in Kubernetes.

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:31.110
Kubernetes is designed to be
agnostic to the configuration

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:32.170
database.

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:35.280
So for that reason, we decided
to have a more generic name

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:36.190
for this feature.

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:37.798
So hopefully this will help you.

00:25:37.798 --> 00:25:39.840
When you're reading
documentation on the feature,

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:41.730
you will see the term
application layer

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:46.240
encryption as opposed to
ETCD level encryption.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:49.710
So now that I check
this option, you

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.500
can see that the UI team
has done a great job.

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:56.040
And what the UI has done
for me in the background

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:58.920
it enumerated all the
keys in that project

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:03.150
that meet the requirements
for this feature.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:04.500
So I only have one.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:07.150
So I'll go ahead
and select that.

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:09.060
And this is another
interesting thing

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:12.900
is that I get a warning here
that some IAM permissions are

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:14.150
missing on the key.

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:17.280
And I will explain what
those IAM permisssions are

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:20.380
and why do we need them when
we go back to the slides.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:22.950
But for now, just say that
the Kubernetes service agent

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:27.510
account needs encrypt/decrypt
privileges on those keys.

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.520
And I have this.

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:32.460
All I need to do is just
click this grant button.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:36.737
And that problem
is now resolved.

00:26:36.737 --> 00:26:39.195
All that I need to do now at
this point is just hit Create.

00:26:41.918 --> 00:26:43.460
And while the cluster
is spinning up,

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:46.530
I would like to switch
back to the slide

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.710
and talk about the feature
under the hood a little bit.

00:26:56.028 --> 00:26:57.000
Thank you.

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:05.070
So let's review the
components involved.

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:06.570
Let's review the
components involved

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:09.237
in the encryption process.

00:27:09.237 --> 00:27:11.070
However, this time we'll
go over [? those ?]

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:14.480
components from the threat
modeling point of view.

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:16.230
To better reason about
the threat model,

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.450
we will classify
components involved

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:22.110
as either volatile, everything
above the dotted line,

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:26.490
or persistent, everything
below the line.

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:32.080
So the persistent resources.

00:27:32.080 --> 00:27:35.860
We assume that attackers
have full access to anything

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:37.750
on a persistent drive.

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:39.460
For the sake of
argument, let's assume

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:44.920
that attackers were able to get
a copy of the Kubernetes master

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:46.620
VM.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.690
Of course, the main
point of interest for us

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.280
is the ETCD data file.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:54.120
However, we are not
limited to-- we are not

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:56.010
assuming that attackers
have access only

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:57.540
to the ETCD data file.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:59.760
Anything on that
persistent drive

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:03.720
which may help attackers to
decrypt secrets also fair game.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:07.610
We need to protect
against those scenarios.

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:11.410
So this introduces the what
I call last mile problem.

00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:14.710
And Seth mentioned Seth calls
it the first secret problem.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:17.890
But they all point
to the same thing.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:20.200
When you look at the diagram,
it becomes fairly obvious

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:24.280
that KMS plugin needs to
authenticate to Cloud KMS

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:25.660
service.

00:28:25.660 --> 00:28:28.240
So this implies that
KMS plugin needs

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:31.970
to have some sort of
credential in order

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:36.648
to negotiate an auth token
to present to Cloud KMS.

00:28:36.648 --> 00:28:38.690
Like pretty much everything
else in Google Cloud,

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:41.690
we use auth protocol
in order to--

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:44.580
for authentication purposes.

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.940
So what if an attacker--

00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:49.880
what if we persisted
the credential

00:28:49.880 --> 00:28:52.420
or the resulting token to disk?

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.830
Well, in this case,
attackers would actually

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:58.070
have everything they need in
order to decrypt our secrets,

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:01.550
because they have access to
the protected ETCD data file

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.340
but they also have a token
or [? its ?] credential

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:05.930
to speak with Cloud KMS.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:08.720
And Cloud KMS, by virtue
of being a cloud service,

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:11.090
obviously is accessible
over public internet.

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:14.320
So it's important to make
sure that we are not leaking--

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:15.320
well, I'm jumping ahead.

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:15.950
Sorry.

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:21.170
So the core design objective is
to ensure in our threat model

00:29:21.170 --> 00:29:25.610
that it is OK for attackers to
have access to the ETCD data

00:29:25.610 --> 00:29:28.040
file, but they should
not have access

00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:30.020
to anything else
that would allow

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:34.220
them to bootstrap communication
with the Cloud KMS.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:35.870
And in the next
several slides, I

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.120
will explain how we
met this objective.

00:29:41.910 --> 00:29:43.510
This will be a review
for most of you.

00:29:43.510 --> 00:29:46.410
But I wanted to make sure
that we are on the same page.

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.805
And I wanted to explain
some concepts about GKE.

00:29:49.805 --> 00:29:51.430
Most of you probably
already know this,

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:55.080
but please allow me a
couple of minutes here.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:58.110
So you probably already
know that Google

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:02.970
handles all of the details of
running Kubernetes masters.

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:05.310
Kubernetes masters
reside in Google

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:09.040
managed infrastructure in
Google managed projects.

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:11.550
However, a less known
fact is that when

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:15.750
you enable GKE API
in your project,

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:19.970
a service account, which
we refer to as Kubernetes,

00:30:19.970 --> 00:30:22.950
[? is ?] Kubernetes
service agent account

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:26.700
is created somewhere else in
Google managed infrastructure.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:31.020
And that service account is
granted some IAM privileges

00:30:31.020 --> 00:30:33.450
in the customer project.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:34.930
Well, why?

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:38.260
Well, when we think about the
process of creating a cluster,

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:41.490
GKE needs to perform
operations such as create

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:44.380
a virtual machine,
create a firewall rule,

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:46.510
create a persistent disk.

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:48.480
Obviously, all of
these operations

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:51.160
require a security context.

00:30:51.160 --> 00:30:54.160
And yes, it is through
this service account

00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:58.490
that GKE accesses resources
in your customer projects.

00:30:58.490 --> 00:30:58.990
OK.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:00.850
So that was just a
review to set the plane

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:02.830
for the next discussion.

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:04.630
So what about Cloud KMS keys?

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:07.270
How do we access those?

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:10.780
Well, you saw in the
demo that the expectation

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:15.550
is that you customers create
those keys ahead of time.

00:31:15.550 --> 00:31:19.150
And you grant the
encrypt/decrypt privileges

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.712
on those keys to Kubernetes
service [? agent ?] account.

00:31:22.712 --> 00:31:24.670
You saw me just clicking
that button and the UI

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:25.880
fixed that for me.

00:31:25.880 --> 00:31:27.560
That was kind of magic.

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.620
But you can certainly do
this in different ways.

00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:32.050
For example, you can go
into the Cloud KMS UI

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:34.540
and just use IAM and
grant permissions there

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:37.600
or you can use gcloud commands.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:42.040
You may be thinking, that
was a very simple operation

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:42.910
to create that key.

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:46.750
Why couldn't you just embed this
into the [? classic ?] creation

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:47.963
process?

00:31:47.963 --> 00:31:49.630
Well, we certainly
considered doing this

00:31:49.630 --> 00:31:51.700
but decided against this.

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:54.850
We felt that giving
GKE the ability

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:58.150
to create this very
security sensitive artifacts

00:31:58.150 --> 00:32:02.330
in your projects would give
GKE way too much power.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:05.440
And we also felt that that
would break the segmentation

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.380
between GKE and Cloud KMS.

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:11.170
Therefore we expect that you
customers create those keys

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:14.650
and allow us, GKE, to
use them for the purposes

00:32:14.650 --> 00:32:15.645
of this feature.

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:21.090
The remaining piece
of the puzzle,

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:25.050
how does KMS plugin
actually authenticate

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.750
to Cloud KMS service?

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:31.590
So this is done by communicating
with an internal token service,

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:33.780
which is again
managed by Google.

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:38.280
And the internal token
service requires KMS plugin

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:42.540
to prove that it is a
member of a cluster that

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:44.990
is linked to the
corresponding service account.

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:48.120
So for example, I'm
trying to negotiate access

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:49.440
to crypto key--

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.960
not me, the Cloud KMS
trying to negotiate access

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:53.760
to that crypto key.

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.920
And what token service
will try to figure out

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:58.765
are you in fact running
on that cluster that

00:32:58.765 --> 00:33:01.140
is linked to that service
account, the Kubernetes service

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:02.340
agent account.

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:04.170
And if you can prove
this to me, then I

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.962
will give you that token.

00:33:06.962 --> 00:33:09.420
So we don't have time to go
through the proof of membership

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:10.920
protocol here.

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:14.100
But it is sufficient to say
that this token service has

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:17.220
full knowledge about
the master components.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:20.970
And it can make that decision
of whether granting or denying

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:25.590
the access based on the prior
information in the database

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:28.910
that it has access to.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:33.220
So assuming that everything
went fine, token service was

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:36.140
satisfied with the fact
that KMS plugin runs

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:39.340
in the right cluster,
it will grant this auth

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:43.330
token which is now KMS
can present to the encrypt

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:44.280
or decrypt-- sorry.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:47.730
It can present to the
Cloud KMS service.

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:50.620
This token service,
this auth token,

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:52.140
is not persistent to disk.

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:55.000
We store it in memory.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:56.890
If the KMS plugin
is rebooted, we'll

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.470
just go through the
same dance once again.

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:03.140
But at no time we actually
restore it to disk.

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:06.900
And that is how we actually--

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:09.900
and that is how we met
our initial objective.

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:13.199
We do not store
credentials on disk.

00:34:13.199 --> 00:34:15.659
And we do not store
the resulting tokens

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:19.409
to disk, which allows us to
meet our threat model where

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:24.719
all that hackers get is just
ETCD with encrypted secrets.

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:28.790
OK.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:30.260
This is my last slide.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:34.480
And I'm going to change the
subject just a little bit.

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:36.440
And I want to talk
about how to run

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:40.280
Cloud KMS plugin outside GKE.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:41.840
So even though we
hope that you're

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:45.050
using GKE to run Kubernetes,
we understand that some of you

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:46.989
may be using Google
Compute Engine

00:34:46.989 --> 00:34:52.190
VMs as the infrastructure
to run Kubernetes.

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:55.909
And the question is can we
ship the same security contract

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:58.010
when running outside GKE?

00:34:58.010 --> 00:34:59.732
And the answer is yes.

00:34:59.732 --> 00:35:01.940
It just simply the catch is
there is simply more work

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:04.760
to manage and set it up.

00:35:04.760 --> 00:35:08.020
Cloud KMS plugin is
developed in open source.

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:11.200
You have a link here
to the repository.

00:35:11.200 --> 00:35:14.080
And in the same repository,
you will find instructions

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:17.650
on how to set up Cloud KMS
plugin outside Kubernetes

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:20.400
when running on GCE.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.338
Architecturally, it
looks very similar,

00:35:23.338 --> 00:35:24.880
the core difference
being, of course,

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:27.420
that there is no Kubernetes
service agent account.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:33.610
Instead, we rely on the service
account in the security context

00:35:33.610 --> 00:35:36.720
of which runs the GCE VM.

00:35:36.720 --> 00:35:39.360
You probably know that
when you create the GCE VM,

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:41.850
you can associate it
with a service account.

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.920
And by virtue of
this association,

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.220
GCE VM can impersonate
that service account.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:49.270
So you pretty much
follow the same process.

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:50.730
You create a service account.

00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:51.990
You create keys.

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:54.690
You grant service account
encrypt/decrypt privileges

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:57.150
on the key, and then
you start the GCE VM

00:35:57.150 --> 00:36:00.753
in the security context
of that service account.

00:36:00.753 --> 00:36:02.670
From that point on,
everything looks the same.

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:04.750
But it's just a
little bit more work.

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:09.180
And then you have to manage
it from that point on.

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:10.530
This is all I had.

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:14.211
With that, I would like to
invite Seth back on stage.

00:36:14.211 --> 00:36:18.190
[APPLAUSE] Thank you.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:19.360
Thank you.

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:20.530
SETH VARGO: All right.

00:36:20.530 --> 00:36:22.360
Thank you, Alex.

00:36:22.360 --> 00:36:24.655
But you work on security, right?

00:36:24.655 --> 00:36:25.780
ALEX TCHERNIAKHOVSKI: Yeah.

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:26.560
SETH VARGO: So
there is one thing

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:28.480
that I learned is that
you never trust someone

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:30.170
who works in security products.

00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:32.687
So how do we know you just
didn't fake that GKE thing?

00:36:32.687 --> 00:36:35.020
Like how do we know that
application layer encryption is

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:36.460
even enabled on that?

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:37.690
ALEX TCHERNIAKHOVSKI: Oh, you
mean like the cluster actually

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:40.090
failed to create [? itself ?]
and I just didn't show that?

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:40.798
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:36:40.798 --> 00:36:41.438
[INAUDIBLE]

00:36:41.438 --> 00:36:43.480
That could have been a
recording for all we know.

00:36:43.480 --> 00:36:44.647
ALEX TCHERNIAKHOVSKI: Right.

00:36:44.647 --> 00:36:47.500
Could we switch
to the demo again?

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:51.570
All right.

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:55.330
So the cluster did create,
which is a good sign.

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:55.830
All right.

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:58.680
But on a serious note,
that was the cluster

00:36:58.680 --> 00:37:01.120
that we created a
few minutes ago.

00:37:01.120 --> 00:37:05.700
So what is the visual indication
that you have a cluster

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:07.282
with that feature enabled?

00:37:07.282 --> 00:37:08.740
So if you scroll
down a little bit,

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:10.772
you will see application
layer encryption.

00:37:10.772 --> 00:37:11.980
And you can see it's enabled.

00:37:11.980 --> 00:37:13.860
And here again you have a link.

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:16.140
If I follow this link,
it should take me

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:21.690
to the cryptographic key that
we created in the first step.

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:25.020
And here's where you can manage
the key, like for example,

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:29.970
the options available to
me are disable, destroy.

00:37:29.970 --> 00:37:33.090
And Seth talked about
crypto-shredding.

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:35.820
If I wanted to kill that cluster
right now, all I need to do

00:37:35.820 --> 00:37:37.530
is just destroy that key.

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:41.580
And essentially, it will not be
possible to create new secrets

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:43.385
on that cluster,
which I'm not sure

00:37:43.385 --> 00:37:44.510
if I recommend this option.

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:46.600
I would probably just delete
the cluster in the first place.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.800
But it is something that
you need to be aware of.

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:51.090
Anyways, I think--
did I prove it enough?

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.430
SETH VARGO: I have another
