00:38:21.820 --> 00:38:26.400
So we know that many of
you might be on prem.

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:28.600
You might be on another
called provider.

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:31.450
You might be doing
all of those things.

00:38:31.450 --> 00:38:33.660
And you may already be
using a secrets management

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:37.350
tool like HashiCorp Vault.

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:40.110
So what if you're not on a cloud
provider Kubernetes service?

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:41.670
What if you're not on GKE?

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:43.770
What if you're not
entirely on GKE?

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:48.390
How can you protect yourself
against these types of attacks

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:51.240
where an attacker has
access to the ETCD backup

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.520
or an online ETCD database?

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:55.630
So you can leverage something
like HashiCorp Vault

00:38:55.630 --> 00:38:56.620
to do this.

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.820
And it looks very similar to
what we already described.

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:02.460
So instead of
Google Cloud KMS, we

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:04.680
throw HashiCorp
Vault in the mix.

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:07.950
Specifically, we're using
HashiCorp Vault's cloud transit

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:11.460
back end, transit secrets
engine if you will.

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:13.710
The secrets engine is very
similar to a Cloud KMS

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:14.640
provider.

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:17.530
It has key management, rotation,
upgrading, downgrading,

00:39:17.530 --> 00:39:18.930
et cetera.

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:21.540
It looks very similar to
what we described before.

00:39:21.540 --> 00:39:24.960
The secret data is given to
the Kubernetes API server.

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:27.420
The Kubernetes API server then
delegates to the encryption

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:28.590
configuration.

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:31.740
The encryption configuration
then makes an API call

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:33.870
to something like
HashiCorp Vault.

00:39:33.870 --> 00:39:37.200
The Vault transit backend
encrypts that data or decrypts

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:40.440
that data before it is
written back to ETCD.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:42.840
So this is a great
opportunity if you

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:44.695
have multi-cloud strategy.

00:39:44.695 --> 00:39:47.070
If you're leveraging multiple
clouds, multiple Kubernetes

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:49.712
clusters, this gives you a
very consistent way in order

00:39:49.712 --> 00:39:52.170
to encrypt your secrets with
an existing secrets management

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:53.470
solution.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.780
So very quickly,
I'd like to show you

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:58.210
what that looks like and show
you what the data in ETCD

00:39:58.210 --> 00:40:00.670
looks like if it's encrypted
with something like HashiCorp

00:40:00.670 --> 00:40:02.440
Vault or Google Cloud KMS.

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:05.680
Can we jump over to the
lappy toppy, please?

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:06.550
Cool.

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:12.310
So what I'm going to do
here is create secret vault.

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:15.817
So I have a minikube cluster,
the same one I had before.

00:40:15.817 --> 00:40:17.650
But this time I added
an encryption provider

00:40:17.650 --> 00:40:20.830
configuration to encrypt
secrets with HashiCorp Vault

00:40:20.830 --> 00:40:22.480
before writing them to ETCD.

00:40:22.480 --> 00:40:25.210
I could have done this Google
CLoud KMS or even another cloud

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:28.000
provider's KMS but I'm using
Vault locally to showcase

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:30.280
a third party open source tool.

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:35.130
Now I'm going to go ahead and
try to access that secret.

00:40:35.130 --> 00:40:38.180
And you'll notice that we don't
get back the plain text data.

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:40.810
So even though I had the same
secret, username seth vargo,

00:40:40.810 --> 00:40:44.110
password secret in leet,
this is what I get back.

00:40:44.110 --> 00:40:47.440
I get back Kubernetes
encryption prefix,

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:49.120
the Vault key that
I'm using, and then

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:53.380
a bunch of data that my terminal
can't even print for you.

00:40:53.380 --> 00:40:55.210
So if an attacker
has access to ETCD

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:58.070
or has access to
this plaintext data,

00:40:58.070 --> 00:40:59.470
this is what they would get.

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:01.012
They don't get the
plaintext secrets.

00:41:01.012 --> 00:41:02.360
They get this encrypted data.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.180
And again, this
example here shows

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:07.870
what it looks like on HashiCorp
Vault. If on Google Cloud

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:10.720
you had access to the
underlying ETCD data,

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.430
it would look very
similar to this

00:41:12.430 --> 00:41:15.160
if you were using
application layer encryption.

00:41:15.160 --> 00:41:18.410
The plaintext data would
not be available to you.

00:41:18.410 --> 00:41:22.620
So we jump back to
the slides real quick.

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:27.390
So to conclude, Kubernetes 1.10
and later introduce some really

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:30.990
great features that make it easy
to be secure with Kubernetes

00:41:30.990 --> 00:41:33.260
secrets.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:35.120
Always use layered encryption.

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:38.270
Use full disk encryption,
operating system encryption,

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:42.200
combined with something like
application layer encryption.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:46.040
Rotate your keys regularly,
both on a schedule

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:48.710
and in the event
of a data breach.

00:41:48.710 --> 00:41:50.390
Leverage envelope encryption.

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:54.690
It's an incredibly scalable
way to perform encryption.

00:41:54.690 --> 00:41:57.153
And fourth, use an
external KMS provider,

00:41:57.153 --> 00:41:59.070
whether that's something
like Google Cloud KMS

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:00.750
or something like
HashiCorp Vault

00:42:00.750 --> 00:42:02.760
as it requires an
attacker to compromise

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:05.580
multiple systems in
order to gain access

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:06.540
to that plaintext data.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:09.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

